eld-out test examples, with both baselines given identical exemplars, decoding, and answer extraction. Because all arms see the *same examples under identical conditions*, differences are attributable to the instruction alone, and paired statistical tests are maximally powerful. A secondary, clearly-labeled exploratory cross-task (transfer) analysis asks whether optimized instructions generalize beyond their tuning task.\n\n**Why a secondary zero-shot empty-prompt arm:** \"Empty prompt\" is ambiguous \u2014 with or without few-shot exemplars. The primary analysis keeps exemplars, extraction, and decoding identical across arms (only the instruction line varies), isolating the instruction's contribution. A fully zero-shot empty-prompt variant is added as a secondary arm so that both readings of the baseline are reported and the conclusion cannot hinge on interpretation.\n\n## 2. Hypotheses\n\n- **H1 (primary):** Per-task OPRO-optimized instructions achieve higher test accuracy than the \"Let's think step by step.\" baseline on the 23-task BBH suite.\n- **H2 (primary):** Per-task OPRO-optimized instructions achieve higher test accuracy than the empty-prompt baseline (with exemplars) on the same suite.\n- **H3 (secondary/exploratory):** OPRO-optimized instructions transfer across tasks (an instruction optimized for task i applied to task j \u2260 i performs no worse than the best baseline on task j). Expected to fail or partially fail; treated as exploratory.\n- **H4 (secondary/descriptive):** Effect heterogeneity: the OPRO advantage varies by task type (e.g., larger on multi-step reasoning tasks, smaller/absent on lexical tasks like snarks).\n\n## 3. Groups / Arms\n\n| Arm | Instruction | Exemplars | Role |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **A** | OPRO-optimized instruction (best-of-validation, per task) | 3-shot CoT (standard BBH exemplars) | Primary treatment |\n| **B** | \"Let's think step by step.\" | Identical 3-shot CoT | Primary baseline 1 |\n| **C** | Empty instruction (\"\") | Identical 3-shot CoT | Primary baseline 2 |\n| **D** | Empty instruction (\"\") | None (zero-shot) | Secondary baseline (ambiguity guard) |\n\nA1/A2/A3 (top-3 validated candidates) are also evaluated per task as a robustness arm for selection-rule sensitivity (\u00a78).\n\n**Controls:** (i) identical test examples, exemplars, temperature (0), max tokens, and extraction/grading code across all arms; (ii) blind, task-agnostic scoring script; (iii) selection of optimized instructions only on data disjoint from test; (iv) multiple seeds for optimizer and evaluation runs; (v) preregistered analysis plan (below) fixed before any evaluation.\n\n## 4. Task and Data Plan\n\n- **Primary:** the standard 23-task BBH suite (\u22486,511 test examples) \u2014 *assumption*, see \u00a710.\n- **Fallback (budget-constrained):** a pre-specified, stratified 10-task subset spanning task categories \u2014 e.g., 2 logical-deduction variants, boolean expressions, causal judgement, date understanding, disambiguation QA, formal fallacies, hyperbaton, movie recommendation, tracking shuffled objects \u2014 fixed in advance, never selected post hoc.\n- **Splits:** per task, use official BBH train/few-shot exemplars for the 3-shot prompts; carve a validation split (~32 examples) from a held-out portion of the task data for OPRO scoring and candidate selection; the remaining full test set is untouched until final evaluation. Where BBH provides no train split (most tasks), draw the validation split randomly from examples excluded from test; randomize the split assignment with a fixed seed and record it.\n- **No leakage:** the optimizer LLM's meta-prompt contains only validation examples and scores; the test set is never shown during optimization or selection.\n\n## 5. OPRO Optimization Protocol (per task; paper-derived defaults)\n\n- Optimizer = evaluator LLM (single model, see \u00a710).\n- Meta-prompt: task description + instruction \"generate a new instruction that outperforms the ones below\" + rolling window of the best ~20 (instruction, accuracy) pairs from optimization history, examples randomly resampled each step.\n- Per step: sample ~8 candidate instructions (temperature \u2248 1.0, 2 samples per meta-prompt configuration).\n- ~20 optimization steps per seed \u2192 ~160 candidates/seed; 2\u20133 optimizer seeds per task (3 primary; 2 if budget-limited).\n- Candidate scoring: accuracy on 32 randomly drawn validation examples per step (redrawn per step to reduce split-specific overfitting); retain the (instruction, score) trajectory.\n- Selection: at the end of optimization, re-score the top-k (k=10) candidates by trajectory score on the *full* validation split; the top 3 become arms A1\u2013A3; A is the single best. This two-stage selection (coarse step-wise screening \u2192 full-validation re-ranking) is the preregistered selection rule.\n- Logging: every candidate, score, and meta-prompt snapshot is recorded for reproducibility and for the convergence robustness check.\n\n## 6. Evaluation Protocol\n\n- For each task, all 4 (+3 robustness) arms are evaluated on the identical full test set (or the fixed 100-example draw in the fallback), temperature 0, identical 3-shot exemplars for A/B/C, identical answer-extraction and exact-match grading.\n- **Repetition:** 3 evaluation runs per arm per task with different seeds controlling example order and any stochastic decoding; since temperature is 0, runs mainly guard against harness nondeterminism and exemplar-order effects; the mean over runs is the arm's task score.\n- Optimizer variance is estimated by treating optimizer seeds as a random factor (A's score per task = mean over seed-wise best instructions).\n\n## 7. Metrics\n\n- **Primary:** per-task exact-match accuracy \u2192 **macro-average over the 23 tasks**, computed for \u0394(A\u2212B) and \u0394(A\u2212C).\n- **Secondary:** per-task accuracies; win/tie/loss counts (A vs. B, A vs. C over 23 tasks); A1\u2013A3 spread (selection sensitivity);